# The annotations of select/select (kit/mkgame.py --part src/select/select.json). Format: ../../../../docs/ann-format.md.
#
# The image: TZX block 4 $7200-$9CF7 (11 000 bytes) as it stands in RAM 1 at $C000 after the setup's relocation pass
# (tools/parts.py reloc()). On the tape its 67 relocatable words are offsets from its first byte; RelocTable $EA0F
# lists where they are. Every address below is the RAM 1 one; on a 48K the same code runs at $7200 (subtract $4E00).
# Witness: c1 (power-on, the load, the selector, Herr Krusche) and explorers (all five explorers, the three devices,
# Help and its pages), work/stage1/*/l1.json. The 48K path is not recorded (work/stage1/NOTES.md): every statement on
# it is known only from the code. "Witness: L1 0 (c1)" lines are placeholders the lead's --fix-witness fills.
# Explorer names are read off the selector picture (SelectorPicture $CF0F) at the regions MoveCursor $C1D5 tests.

@equ $1601 ChanOpen ; the ROM's CHAN-OPEN: A = the stream
@equ $3C00 RomCharSet at $C29A,$C4C5 ; the ROM character set less 256 (CHARS' usual value): glyph = $3C00 + 8 x code
@equ $4000 Screen 6144 ; the display file
@equ $5800 Attributes 768 ; the screen attributes
@equ $5B70 Explorer ; the explorer index: 0-4, $FE on the Help box, $FF elsewhere while the selector runs
@equ $5BA6 DigitBuffer 3 ; three ASCII digits for PrintNumber3 $C4D7 (never executed)
@equ $5C36 Chars ; system variable CHARS
@equ $5C3A ErrNr at $C192 ; system variable ERR_NR, the ROM's IY base
@equ $5FE5 Last7FFD ; the last value written to port $7FFD (the IM 2 handler $9491 restores it)
@equ $5FE6 PagingStub 21 ; the copy of PagedReadStub $C4A6 the selector reads the banks through
@equ $5FFE Has128K ; $11 on a 128K, 0 on a 48K
@equ $5FFF GameLoaded ; 0 from the loader, 1 once GameEntry $8F00 has run
@equ $6000 ExplorerSet 4608 at $C15F,$C185 ; the chosen explorer's data set in play
@equ $9CF8 TapeExplorerSets at $C045,$C17B ; block 4's five explorer data sets as loaded, $1200 bytes each

@isub $C01D LD HL,$C000
@isub $C048 LD DE,$C000
@isub $C06C LD DE,$C000
@isub $C092 LD HL,$6000
@isub $C098 LD DE,$C000
@isub $C10C LD HL,80*256+100
@isub $C13F LD HL,$C000
@isub $C145 LD HL,$D200
@isub $C14B LD HL,$E400
@isub $C153 LD HL,$C000
@isub $C159 LD HL,$D200
@isub $C1AD LD BC,SelectorPicture

@module setup ; The 128K setup: the paging test, the copies to RAM 1, 3, 4 and 6
@about The loader $5D08 calls the block at $7200 with BC = $7200. Setup $C005 reads the first byte: 0 means the code is not relocated yet. Run at $7200 it tests the paging: $AA written to $C000 with RAM 0 paged, $55 with RAM 1, then read back with RAM 0; equal bytes mean one RAM there, a 48K. On a 128K it copies the block from $7200 to RAM 1 at $C000, the explorer sets 0-2 to RAM 4 ($C000, $D200, $E400) and 3-4 to RAM 3 ($C000, $D200), the music player $6000-$71FF to RAM 6, sets Has128K $5FFE = $11 and jumps to the copy in RAM 1 with BC = $C000, where the relocation pass runs. On a 48K it relocates the code in place at $7200.
@about c1: the setup runs at $7200 at frame 11841 (the L1 arrival: $C000 in RAM 1 written by the LDIR at $7243, entered from $72A7); the copy to RAM 6 is the LDIR at $729B.

$C000 c SelectorStart ; The block's first bytes: the relocated flag, a jump over the table offset
> The NOP at $C000 is also a flag: the tape holds 0, RelocateSelector $C0AB writes $2F (CPL) once the words are relocated, so every later entry runs CPL; JR (harmless) and Setup $C005 sees a nonzero byte. Entered with BC = the block's address: by the loader at $7200 (CALL $7200 at $5D61), by Setup at $C000 in RAM 1 (JP at $C0A7), and by the game's menu (CALL $C000 at $8F5E with RAM 1 paged, Last7FFD = $11).
> c1: entered twice (the load at frame 11841, the game's menu at frame 22215); explorers: six times (the menu after each abort).
> Witness: L1 8 (c1, explorers).
$C003 w RelocTableOffset 1 ; The offset of RelocTable from the block's start ($2A0F)
> RelocateSelector $C0AB adds this word to the block's address to find RelocTable $EA0F. It is not itself relocated.

$C005 c Setup ; Dispatch on the relocated flag and the run address; on a 128K the paging test and the copies
> In: BC = the block's address. HL = BC; a nonzero first byte (relocated) goes to SelectorEntry $C0D2. With B = $C0 (the unrelocated copy in RAM 1) it goes to RelocateSelector $C0AB. Otherwise (run at $7200 by the loader) the paging test, using the alternate registers for the port so that BC and HL keep the address: Has128K $5FFE = 0; RAM 0 at $C000 ($7FFD = $10, ROM 1), its byte at $C000 kept in E and $AA written; RAM 1 ($11), $55 written and read; RAM 0 again and compared; the byte restored. Equal: a 48K, RelocateInPlace $C0AA.
> 128K: RAM 1 paged, the $2EE0 bytes from $7200 copied to $C000 (the block's 11 000 and 1000 bytes of set 0 after it, unused there); the 3 x $1200 bytes from $9CF8 to RAM 4 $C000 a byte at a time (RAM 0 paged to read, since the source runs past $C000, RAM 4 to write), the 2 x $1200 bytes after them to RAM 3 $C000 the same way, Has128K = $11, the music player $6000-$71FF ($1200 bytes) to RAM 6 $C000, RAM 1 paged again, and JP $C000 with BC = $C000.
> c1: one run at frame 11841: 13 824 bytes to RAM 4 ($724E loop), 9216 to RAM 3 ($7272 loop), 4608 to RAM 6 ($729B). explorers starts from a snapshot after the load: only the relocated path ($C009 to $C068).
> Witness: L1 8 (c1, explorers).
$C009 ; relocated (first byte nonzero): the selector proper
$C00B ; not relocated: B = $C0 is the copy in RAM 1, relocate it there
$C010 TestPaging ; run at $7200 by the loader: the paging test with the port in the alternate BC
$C015 ; Has128K = 0 until the test says otherwise
$C01D ; the test byte: $C000, in whichever RAM is paged there
$C030 ; $55 read under RAM 1 against RAM 0's byte: equal on a 48K
$C031 ; put RAM 0's byte back
$C033 ; a 48K: relocate in place at $7200
$C035 Copy128K ; a 128K: RAM 1 at $C000, copy the block there
$C043 ; 11 000 bytes of the block and 1000 of set 0 after it
$C045 ; explorer sets 0-2 ($3600 bytes) to RAM 4
$C04E CopyToRam4Loop ; read with RAM 0 paged, write with RAM 4 paged
$C068 SetupToEntry ; a JR stepping stone to SelectorEntry
$C06A SetupToRelocate ; a JR stepping stone to RelocateSelector
$C06C CopyToRam3 ; explorer sets 3-4 ($2400 bytes) to RAM 3
$C072 CopyToRam3Loop ; read with RAM 0 paged, write with RAM 3 paged
$C086 ; Has128K = $11
$C08E ; the music player $6000-$71FF to RAM 6 at $C000
$C09D ; RAM 1 again
$C0A4 ; enter the copy in RAM 1 with BC = its address

@module relocation ; The relocation pass and its table
@about The tape holds every absolute address inside the block as an offset from the block's first byte. RelocateSelector $C0AB adds the run address (BC: $C000 in RAM 1 on a 128K, $7200 on a 48K) to each word RelocTable $EA0F lists (67 words, a zero word ends the table) and marks the block relocated ($2F at its first byte). Addresses outside the block (the paging stub, the explorer sets in RAM 3 and 4, $C000 as the paging slot) are absolute and not listed. tools/parts.py reloc() does the same for the case's image, tools/mktape.py the inverse for the tape.

$C0AA c RelocateInPlace ; The 48K path: back to the main registers, relocate at $7200
> Reached from Setup $C005 (JR Z at $C033) when the paging test found one RAM: EXX brings back HL = BC = $7200 and falls into RelocateSelector. Known only from the code (the 48K path is not recorded).
> Witness: L1 0 ().
$C0AB c RelocateSelector ; Add the run address to every word RelocTable lists, mark the block relocated
> In: HL = BC = the block's address. Writes $2F at the first byte, reads RelocTableOffset $C003 (+3), then for each nonzero offset in RelocTable adds BC to the word at block + offset. Falls into SelectorEntry $C0D2 at the zero word.
> c1: run once at $C000 in RAM 1 (frame 11841): 68 table words read, 67 words relocated.
> Witness: L1 1 (c1).
$C0B9 RelocateLoop ; next offset; zero ends the table
$C0C3 ; HL = the word's address, add the base to the word

@module selector ; The explorer selector: the picture, the hand cursor, the choice and the copy of the chosen set
@about SelectorEntry $C0D2 copies PagedReadStub $C4A6 to PagingStub $5FE6. On a 128K the first entry (GameLoaded $5FFF = 0, still inside the loader) only patches the stub to OUT (C),A; RET and runs it with A = $10: RAM 0 is paged at $C000 and the RET returns to the loader, which loads the game. Every later entry (the game's menu, $8F5E) and the 48K entry run the selector: the picture SelectorPicture $CF0F, a 24 x 16 hand cursor moved 2 pixels a frame by the keyboard (Z X O K, SPACE), a Kempston joystick (port $1F) or Sinclair (6 7 8 9 0), all three polled every pass (ReadAnyDevice $C3BF). MoveCursor $C1D5 sets Explorer $5B70 from the cursor's place: 0 Fortisque-Smithe, 1 Herr Krusche, 2 Henri Beaucoup, 3 Big John Caine, 4 Wu Pong, $FE the Help box, $FF elsewhere. FIRE on an explorer copies that explorer's data set to ExplorerSet $6000 (CopyExplorerSet $C134) and returns; FIRE on Help shows the instructions (ShowHelp $C536).
@about A 48K never pages: the setup relocated the block to $7200 and the loader's CALL $7200 runs it there before the game loads. After STOP THE TAPE / THEN PRESS SPACE KEY (ShowStopTape $C295) the selector runs; the choice copies the set from the tape's copy at $9CF8 to $6000, prints EXPLORER SELECTED / START TAPE and returns to the loader, which loads the game over the block and the sets. The game's menu does not call the selector on a 48K (Has128K = 0 at $8F4A), so the choice is final (the 48K Help text says so). Known only from the code.

$C0D2 c SelectorEntry ; Copy the paging stub to $5FE6; on a 128K's first entry page RAM 0 and return to the loader
> Copies the 21 bytes of PagedReadStub $C4A6 to PagingStub $5FE6 on every entry. Has128K $5FFE nonzero and GameLoaded $5FFF zero: the first entry, from the loader through Setup and RelocateSelector. Then $5FE6-$5FE7 = $ED,$79 (OUT (C),A) and $5FE8 = $C9 (RET), BC = $7FFD, A = $10 and JP $5FE6: RAM 0 at $C000, ROM 1, and the RET returns to the loader's CALL $7200 (to $5D64), whose stack still holds that address. Otherwise SelectorRun $C0FC.
> c1: the first entry at frame 11841 takes the patch ($C0E9-$C0F9 once; $5FE6 then runs OUT and RET once); the second (frame 22215, from the game) runs the selector. explorers: six entries, all to the selector.
> Witness: L1 8 (c1, explorers).
$C0E9 ; a 128K inside the loader: make the stub OUT (C),A; RET
$C0EC ; $ED,$79: OUT (C),A
$C0F2 ; RET after it
$C0F7 ; RAM 0, ROM 1: the RET goes back to the loader at $5D64
$C0FC SelectorRun ; clear the screen; on a 48K ask to stop the tape first
$C106 SelectorRestart ; Help returns here: clear, draw the picture, the cursor at x 100, y 80
$C112 SelectorLoop ; draw the cursor, read the devices, move, classify
$C118 ; FIRE (bit 4)?
$C11C ; on nothing: go on
$C123 ; on the Help box
$C128 ; on an explorer
$C12A SelectorWait ; IY = 0 for the ROM's IM 1 handler of the 48K path, wait a frame, restore under the cursor

$C134 c CopyExplorerSet ; Copy the chosen explorer's data set ($1200 bytes) to ExplorerSet $6000
> Reached from SelectorLoop $C112 with FIRE on an explorer (Explorer $5B70 = 0-4). 128K: the set's bank and address: 0 RAM 4 $C000, 1 RAM 4 $D200, 2 RAM 4 $E400, 3 RAM 3 $C000, 4 RAM 3 $D200 (where Setup $C005 put them); each byte is read through PagingStub $5FE6 (A = the bank, HL = the address; RAM 1 is paged back after each byte) and stored at $6000 up. RET returns to the game's menu ($8F61), which starts the game.
> 48K (CopySet48K $C175): the set at $9CF8 + $1200 x Explorer, where the tape put it, copied by LDIR; then stream 2 opened and StartTapeText $C2E8 printed by RST $10 up to its $0D, and RET to the loader at $5D64 with interrupts on. Known only from the code.
> c1: explorer 1 (Herr Krusche), 4608 stub calls; explorers: all five, each branch of the bank and address chain $C13F-$C159 taken.
> Witness: L1 6 (c1, explorers).
$C13A ; E = the bank, HL = the set's address in it
$C145 ; explorer 1
$C14B ; explorer 2
$C151 ; explorers 3 and 4 are in RAM 3
$C159 ; explorer 4
$C15C CopySetFromBank ; $1200 bytes through the stub to $6000
$C163 CopySetLoop ; one byte: page RAM E, read, page RAM 1
$C175 CopySet48K ; a 48K: the set from the tape's copy at $9CF8
$C181 SetAddressLoop ; HL = $9CF8 + $1200 x Explorer
$C185 CopySetLdir
$C18A ; stream 2, the upper screen
$C196 PrintStartTape ; print up to the $0D with the ROM's RST $10

$C1A2 c RestoreUnderCursor ; Put back the picture's bytes under the cursor (3 bytes x 16 rows)
> From CursorScreen $C4A4 (the screen address DrawCursor $C314 stored), each of 16 pixel rows: the 3 bytes at the same place in SelectorPicture $CF0F (address - $4000 + $CF0F) copied to the screen, then one row down (PixelDown $C35F). Called by SelectorWait $C12A after the HALT, before the next DrawCursor.
> c1: 1126 calls (frames 22225-23350).
> Witness: L1 3452 (c1, explorers).
$C1A8 RestoreRowLoop ; the picture's byte: the screen address with bit 14 off, plus SelectorPicture

$C1C9 c DrawSelectorPicture ; Copy SelectorPicture (6912 bytes) to the screen
> LDIR of SelectorPicture $CF0F to $4000-$5AFF. Called by SelectorRestart $C106. c1: once, frame 22221.
> Witness: L1 7 (c1, explorers).

$C1D5 c MoveCursor ; Read the devices, move the cursor 2 pixels, set Explorer from its place
> Out: E = the device bits (ReadAnyDevice $C3BF: bit 0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up, 4 FIRE); CursorXY $C4A2 moved 2 pixels a step within x 0-$E8 and y 0-$A8 (L = x, H = y: the cursor's top left); Explorer $5B70 set: 0 Fortisque-Smithe (y < $4D, $4C <= x < $91), 1 Herr Krusche ($0C <= y < $57, x >= $BC), 2 Henri Beaucoup ($6E <= y < $A9, $B0 <= x < $DB), 3 Big John Caine (x < $36, $3C <= y < $7D), 4 Wu Pong ($52 <= x < $7F, y >= $86), $FE Help ($0A <= x < $22, y >= $8C), else $FF; the tests in that order, the first that holds. Then three calls to ShowCoords $C4D6 (Explorer at $4040, x at $4000, y at $4020), which returns at once.
> c1: 1127 calls; explorers: every region reached ($C219, $C22A, $C23F, $C250, $C261, $C273 taken).
> Witness: L1 3459 (c1, explorers).
$C1E7 MoveLeft ; bit 1: x - 2 unless 0
$C1F1 MoveDown ; bit 2: y + 2 below $A8
$C1FC MoveUp ; bit 3: y - 2 unless 0
$C206 ClassifyCursor ; C = 0: Fortisque-Smithe's box
$C21B TryKrusche ; C = 1
$C22C TryBeaucoup ; C = 2
$C241 TryCaine ; C = 3
$C252 TryWuPong ; C = 4
$C263 TryHelp ; C = $FE: the Help box
$C275 OnNothing ; C = $FF
$C276 StoreExplorer ; Explorer = C; the coordinate read-out (disabled)

$C295 c ShowStopTape ; The 48K's first screen: STOP THE TAPE / THEN PRESS SPACE KEY, wait for SPACE
> Opens stream 2, sets Chars $5C36 to $3C00 (the ROM font), prints StopTapeText $C2B8 by RST $10 up to its $0D, then waits until SPACE (port $7FFE bit 0) is down. Called by SelectorRun $C0FC only when Has128K $5FFE = 0: not executed in any recording (c1, explorers: 128K). Known only from the code.
> Witness: L1 0 ().
$C2A3 PrintStopTape ; RST $10 up to the $0D
$C2AE WaitSpace ; until SPACE is down

$C2B8 b StopTapeText 8 ; The 48K's STOP THE TAPE / THEN PRESS SPACE KEY: ROM print codes, $0D ends
> For RST $10: INK 8, PAPER 8 (transparent), AT 10,9, FLASH 1, "STOP THE TAPE", FLASH 0, AT 12,6, "THEN PRESS SPACE KEY", $0D (the end mark ShowStopTape $C295 tests, not printed). Not read in any recording (128K).
@res texts/stop-tape.bin
$C2C1 ; "STOP THE TAPE"
$C2CE ; FLASH 0, AT 12,6
$C2D3 ; "THEN PRESS SPACE KEY", $0D
$C2E8 b StartTapeText 8 ; The 48K's EXPLORER SELECTED / START TAPE: ROM print codes, $0D ends
> For RST $10 (PrintStartTape $C196): FLASH 0, INK 8, PAPER 8, AT 1,1, "EXPLORER", AT 2,1, "SELECTED", AT 5,0, FLASH 1, "START TAPE", $0D. Printed over the picture at the top left. Not read in any recording (128K).
@res texts/start-tape.bin
$C2F1 ; "EXPLORER", AT 2,1
$C2FC ; "SELECTED", AT 5,0, FLASH 1
$C309 ; "START TAPE", $0D

$C314 c DrawCursor ; Draw the hand cursor at CursorXY, masked, from the frame for its pixel shift
> PixelToScreen $C39C gives BC = the screen address of CursorXY $C4A2 and A = x AND 6; BC is kept in CursorScreen $C4A4 for RestoreUnderCursor $C1A2. The frame: CursorSprites $CD8F + $60 x (A / 2), four frames pre-shifted 0, 2, 4, 6 pixels. Three columns of 16 rows (DrawColumn16 $C34B): screen = (screen AND mask) OR graphic, the graphic the frame's first 48 bytes, the mask the next 48.
> c1: 1127 calls; explorers: all four frames ($C333 taken 43 times).
> Witness: L1 3459 (c1, explorers).
$C325 ; x AND 6 = 2
$C32C ; 4
$C333 ; 6
$C336 CursorFrameChosen ; DE = the mask, 48 bytes after the graphic
$C33F DrawColumnLoop ; three columns, BC one byte right each

$C34B c DrawColumn16 ; Draw 16 rows of one cursor column: two DrawColumn8
> The chain $C34B, $C34E, $C351, $C354 each call the next and fall into it, so PlotCursorByte $C357 runs 16, 8, 4, 2 times from each entry. c1: 3381 calls (1127 x 3 columns).
> Witness: L1 10377 (c1, explorers).
$C34E c DrawColumn8 ; 8 rows: two DrawColumn4
> Witness: L1 20754 (c1, explorers).
$C351 c DrawColumn4 ; 4 rows: two DrawColumn2
> Witness: L1 41508 (c1, explorers).
$C354 c DrawColumn2 ; 2 rows: two PlotCursorByte
> Witness: L1 83016 (c1, explorers).
$C357 c PlotCursorByte ; One cursor byte: (BC) = (BC) AND (DE) OR (HL), then a row down
> HL = the graphic, DE = the mask, BC = the screen; HL and DE step on and the code falls into PixelDown $C35F. c1: 54 096 runs (3381 x 16).
> Witness: L1 166032 (c1, explorers).
$C35F c PixelDown ; BC = the screen address one pixel row down
> The usual display-file walk: B + 1; across a character row back to its top and C + $20; across a third (C's top three bits all set) C AND $1F and B + 8. Used by PlotCursorByte $C357 and RestoreUnderCursor $C1A2.
> Witness: L1 221264 (c1, explorers).
$C364 ; a character row crossed
$C374 PixelDownThird ; the next third

$C37F c ClearScreen ; Black border, the bitmap cleared, attributes $47 (bright white on black)
> Called by SelectorRun $C0FC and SelectorRestart $C106. c1: twice (frames 22215-22221).
> Witness: L1 14 (c1, explorers).

$C39C c PixelToScreen ; Pixel x (L), y (H) to the screen address BC and the pixel shift A
> Out: BC = $4000 + the display-file address of the byte holding pixel (x, y); A = x AND 6 (x is always even); flags from the AND. Used by DrawCursor $C314.
> Witness: L1 3459 (c1, explorers).

@module input ; The selector's input: the keyboard, Kempston and Sinclair joystick, polled together
@about ReadAnyDevice $C3BF tries the keyboard (SPACE, Z, X, O, K), then a Kempston joystick if 256 reads of port $1F never show bits 5-7 (no Kempston: a floating bus reads them set), then Sinclair (6 7 8 9 0), and returns the first that has a direction or FIRE. The bits are the Kempston's: 0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up, 4 FIRE; ReadDeviceFiltered $C3EC drops a reading with both left and right or both up and down. The game's own device menu ($93AF) is not used here. A cursor-key reader (5 6 7 8 0, ReadCursorKeys $C444) sits in the code but no path reaches it.

$C3BD b InputBits 2 ; The device bits ReadDevice builds, and the device it reads
> InputBits: bit 0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up, 4 FIRE; InputDevice $C3BE: 0 keyboard, 1 Kempston, 2 Sinclair (ReadAnyDevice $C3BF sets it). explorers writes both ($C3BD-$C3BE).
$C3BE InputDevice ; 0 keyboard, 1 Kempston, 2 Sinclair

$C3BF c ReadAnyDevice ; Poll the keyboard, a Kempston (when present), then Sinclair; A = the first nonzero reading
> Out: A = the bits (0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up, 4 FIRE), NZ when any; InputDevice $C3BE = the device read last. Kempston presence: port $1F ORed over 256 reads, bits 5-7 must stay 0. After the Sinclair read InputBits is cleared (A keeps the reading). Called by MoveCursor $C1D5.
> c1: 1127 calls, 796 reach Sinclair (the Kempston moved the cursor there); explorers: keyboard FIRE and right ($C477, $C488), Sinclair left and FIRE ($C424, $C41E) taken.
> Witness: L1 3459 (c1, explorers).
$C3C8 ; the keyboard gave nothing: Kempston, if one answers
$C3D1 KempstonProbe ; port $1F ORed over 256 reads
$C3E1 TrySinclair ; Sinclair, the last; InputBits cleared after

$C3EC c ReadDeviceFiltered ; ReadDevice, with left+right or up+down taken as nothing
> Out: A = E = the bits, or A = 0 when bits 0 and 1 or bits 2 and 3 are both set.
> Witness: L1 9690 (c1, explorers).
$C3F8 NoLeftRight ; up and down together?
$C401 ReadingOk ; A = the reading

$C403 c ReadDevice ; Read InputDevice's keys or port into InputBits; A = the bits
> HL = InputBits $C3BD, cleared, then by InputDevice $C3BE: 0 ReadKeyboard $C46E, 1 ReadKempston $C43C (port $1F AND $1F, already in this order), 2 the Sinclair keys on half row $EFFE: 0 FIRE, 6 left, 7 right, 8 down, 9 up. All end at InputDone $C4A0 (A = InputBits).
> Witness: L1 9690 (c1, explorers).
$C413 ReadSinclair ; half row 6-0 (port $EFFE), a pressed key reads 0
$C420 SinclairLeft ; 6: left
$C426 SinclairRight ; 7: right
$C42C SinclairUp ; 9: up
$C432 SinclairDown ; 8: down
$C43C ReadKempston ; port $1F: bits 0-4 as they are

$C444 c ReadCursorKeys ; The cursor keys 5 6 7 8 and 0 to InputBits: no path reaches it
> Port $F7FE bit 4 (5) left; port $EFFE bit 0 (0) FIRE, bit 2 (8) right, bit 3 (7) up, bit 4 (6) down; ends at InputDone $C4A0. ReadDevice $C403 has no device number for it and nothing jumps here (the bytes after ReadKempston's JP); its JP operand $C46C is in RelocTable, so it was assembled as live code. Not executed in any recording (c1, explorers); known only from the code.
> Witness: L1 0 ().
$C44F CursorFire
$C45A CursorRight
$C460 CursorUp
$C466 CursorDown

$C46E c ReadKeyboard ; The keyboard to InputBits: SPACE FIRE, Z left, X right, O up, K down
> Ports $7FFE (SPACE), $FEFE (Z, X), $DFFE (O), $BFFE (K); a pressed key reads 0. InputDone $C4A0 returns A = InputBits; ReadSinclair, ReadKempston and ReadCursorKeys jump there too.
> Witness: L1 3459 (c1, explorers).
$C479 KeyLeft ; Z, X
$C484 KeyRight
$C48A KeyUp ; O: up
$C495 KeyDown ; K: down
$C4A0 InputDone ; A = InputBits

@module selector
$C4A2 w CursorXY 1 ; The cursor's place (L = x, H = y) and its screen address
> CursorXY: x 0-$E8 and y 0-$A8, the top left of the 24 x 16 hand; SelectorRestart $C106 sets x 100, y 80 ($5064), MoveCursor $C1D5 moves it. CursorScreen $C4A4: the screen address DrawCursor $C314 drew at, read by RestoreUnderCursor $C1A2. explorers writes $C4A2-$C4A5.
$C4A4 CursorScreen ; the screen address of the drawn cursor

@module paging ; The paging stub
@about The selector runs in RAM 1 at $C000, so it cannot page RAM 3 or 4 there itself: SelectorEntry $C0D2 copies PagedReadStub $C4A6 to PagingStub $5FE6, below $6000, and CopyExplorerSet $C134 calls it once a byte. Every OUT is preceded by a store to Last7FFD $5FE5, the value the game's IM 2 handler $9491 puts back after the music.

$C4A6 c PagedReadStub ; Read the byte at HL in RAM A, then page RAM 1 back (run as a copy at $5FE6)
> In: A = the RAM (3 or 4), HL = the address ($C000-$FFFF). Out: A = the byte; RAM 1 paged at $C000 again; BC = $7FFD. Port $7FFD gets A OR $10 (ROM 1, the 48 BASIC ROM), then $11. Never run here: SelectorEntry copies these 21 bytes to $5FE6 on every entry, and CopyExplorerSet $C134 calls $5FE6. On a 128K's first entry $5FE6-$5FE8 are patched to OUT (C),A; RET before the jump there.
> c1: $5FE6 runs 4609 times (4608 calls from $C165, one jump from $C0F9).
> Witness: L1 0 ().

@module print ; Glyph print and the disabled number read-out
@about PrintGlyph $C4BB prints one character of the ROM font for the Help pages. ShowCoords $C4D6 is a RET in front of PrintNumber3 $C4D7, a three-digit print MoveCursor $C1D5 calls three times a pass: the author's read-out of Explorer and the cursor's x and y at the top left of the screen, switched off by that RET.

$C4BB c PrintGlyph ; Print the ROM-font glyph of A at screen address HL; HL + 1
> The 8 bytes at $3C00 + 8 x A copied down the character cell (D + 1 a row). BC, DE kept; HL returns + 1. Called by PrintPage $C574 for each character, and by PrintNumber3 $C4D7 and PrintDigit $C52C (never executed).
> explorers: 3878 calls (the Help pages).
> Witness: L1 3878 (explorers).
$C4CB GlyphRowLoop ; 8 rows

$C4D6 c ShowCoords ; RET: the coordinate read-out switched off
> MoveCursor $C1D5 calls it three times a pass with A = Explorer, x, y and DE = $4040, $4000, $4020. The RET stops it in front of PrintNumber3 $C4D7, which would print A in decimal at DE.
> c1: 3381 runs (1127 x 3).
> Witness: L1 10377 (c1, explorers).

$C4D7 c PrintNumber3 ; Print A (0-255) as three decimal digits at DE, leading zeros as spaces after the number
> DigitBuffer $5BA6-$5BA8 gets the hundreds, tens and units as ASCII by repeated subtraction; the leading "0" digits are skipped (B counts them) and as many spaces printed after the digits (PrintGlyph $C4BB), so the field is always three characters. AF kept. Its CALL operands are in RelocTable, so it was live code once; ShowCoords' RET at $C4D6 cuts it off. Not executed in any recording (c1, explorers); known only from the code.
> Witness: L1 0 ().
$C4DD HundredsLoop
$C4E6 TensDigit
$C4E9 TensLoop
$C4F2 UnitsDigit
$C4F5 UnitsLoop
$C4FD SkipLeadingZeros
$C510 PrintThreeDigits
$C515 PrintTwoDigits
$C51A PrintLastDigit
$C523 PadSpaces ; one space per zero skipped
$C52A PrintNumberDone

$C52C c PrintDigit ; Print the digit at (HL) at screen address DE
> PrintGlyph $C4BB with HL and DE kept. Called only by PrintNumber3 $C4D7: not executed in any recording.
> Witness: L1 0 ().

@module help ; Help: the 48K warning and the instructions, four text pages
@about ShowHelp $C536 runs on FIRE over the Help box. A 48K first shows Help48KText $C5F9 (choose now, restart the tape; a new explorer means reloading); ENTER goes back to the selector, SPACE on. Then the instructions: HelpTitle $C708 (TERRAMEX INSTRUCTIONS, about 2.9 s), HelpPage1 $C721 (the story), HelpPage2 $C97F (the quest), HelpPage3 $CB7D (the bearers and the keys). SPACE turns a page (after page 3 back to the title), ENTER returns to the selector (SelectorRestart $C106).
@about PrintPage $C574 draws a page on a cleared screen (blue border and paper, white ink) from bytes: $16 column, row = position; $0D = the next row, column 0; $01 = pause about 10.2 M T-states (6 x 65 536 turns of a 26 T loop) and return; $00 = wait for SPACE (A = 1, NZ) or ENTER (A = 0, Z) and return; any other byte = a ROM-font glyph, the column wrapping at 32.

$C536 c ShowHelp ; Show the Help pages until ENTER, then back to the selector
> Reached by JP from SelectorLoop $C112 with FIRE on the Help box (Explorer $5B70 = $FE). Has128K $5FFE = 0: Help48KText $C5F9 first, then the keyboard must be released. Then the title and page 1, page 2, page 3 in a loop while SPACE answers; ENTER at any page jumps to SelectorRestart $C106.
> explorers: once (frames 13 750-15 190): the title and page 1 three times, pages 2 and 3 twice each.
> Witness: L1 1 (explorers).
$C543 WaitKeysUp ; until no key is down
$C550 HelpPagesLoop ; the title (pause), page 1
$C55D HelpReturn ; ENTER: back to the selector
$C560 ; page 2
$C569 ; page 3; SPACE repeats from the title

$C574 c PrintPage ; Clear the screen to blue and print a page of text bytes; A = 1 for SPACE, 0 for ENTER
> In: HL = the page. Border 1, bitmap $4000-$57FF cleared, attributes $0F; B = row, C = column from 0, 0. The byte codes: $16 c r position, $0D next row, $01 pause and return (A = 0), $00 wait for SPACE or ENTER and return, else PrintGlyph $C4BB at H = $40 OR (B AND $18), L = (B x 32 AND $E0) OR C, the column wrapping at 32. Out: A = 1 (NZ) for SPACE, 0 (Z) for ENTER or after a pause.
> explorers: 10 calls, 3878 glyphs, 3 pauses.
> Witness: L1 10 (explorers).
$C596 PageNextByte
$C5A2 PageControl ; not a position
$C5A6 ; $01: pause and return
$C5AB PauseLoop ; 6 x 65 536 turns of 26 T-states
$C5B3 PageEndOrCr ; $00 ends the page
$C5BA ; $0D: next row
$C5BF PrintPageGlyph ; a glyph at row B, column C
$C5E2 PageGlyphDone ; next byte
$C5E4 WaitSpaceOrEnter ; SPACE: A = 1; ENTER: A = 0
$C5F7 SpacePressed

$C5F9 t Help48KText ; Help on a 48K: choose now, restart the tape; SPACE goes on, ENTER returns
> PrintPage $C574 text: " As you have a 48K machine you must choose your explorer now and then load in the rest of the program. Restart the tape when you have chosen your explorer. You will have to switch off and reload the tape to choose a new explorer.", then at column 0, row 23 "<SPACE> Continue <ENTER> Returns" and $00. Not read in any recording (128K); known only from the code.
@res texts/help-48k.bin
$C708 t HelpTitle ; The title card: TERRAMEX INSTRUCTIONS at column 5, row 10, then $01 (pause)
> explorers reads it three times (ShowHelp $C536).
@res texts/help-title.bin
$C721 t HelpPage1 ; Instructions page 1: Professor Albert Eyestrain, the asteroid, his formula
> Professor Albert Eyestrain warned twenty years ago of a gigantic asteroid; ridiculed, he vanished, leaving a formula: "who finds it will be able to find me". The asteroid now hits within a month. Ends with "<SPACE> Continue <ENTER> Returns" at row 23 and $00.
@res texts/help-page1.bin
$C97F t HelpPage2 ; Instructions page 2: find the professor; 5 explorers; the VAC
> Find the professor and convince him to save the world; you are one of 5 explorers in a desert mountain range with strange creatures and things that "do not behave normally"; a VAC flies over power sources in the ground ("Guess who made it ?").
@res texts/help-page2.bin
$CB7D t HelpPage3 ; Instructions page 3: the invisible bearers and the keys
> Your invisible bearers carry things, but to use something you must carry it yourself. Keys: Z left, X right, O up, K down, SPACE jump; S swap the item with the nearest; 1 move bearers and objects right, 2 left; CAPS SHIFT + Q quits and restarts; the joystick is chosen on the title page; here up, down, left, right move the hand and FIRE selects. Ends with "<SPACE> Repeats <ENTER> Returns" and $00.
@res texts/help-page3.bin

@module selector
$CD8F b CursorSprites 16 ; The hand cursor: 4 frames pre-shifted 0, 2, 4, 6 pixels, 24 x 16, graphic then mask
> Each frame is 96 bytes: the graphic, 3 columns of 16 bytes (column-major, top to bottom), then the mask in the same order (AND before OR). DrawCursor $C314 picks the frame by x AND 6. explorers reads all four.
@res graphics/cursor.bin
$CDBF CursorMask0 ; frame 0's mask
$CDEF CursorFrame2 ; shifted 2 pixels
$CE1F CursorMask2
$CE4F CursorFrame4 ; shifted 4 pixels
$CE7F CursorMask4
$CEAF CursorFrame6 ; shifted 6 pixels
$CEDF CursorMask6
$CF0F b SelectorPicture 32 ; The selector screen: the five explorers' portraits and names, the Help box (6912 bytes)
> A whole screen ($1800 bitmap, $300 attributes): Fortisque-Smithe (top middle), Herr Krusche (top right), Big John Caine (left), Henri Beaucoup (bottom right), Wu Pong (bottom middle) and a "?" Help box (bottom left), each named under the portrait. DrawSelectorPicture $C1C9 copies it to the screen; RestoreUnderCursor $C1A2 reads it back under the cursor.
@res screens/selector.bin
$E70F SelectorAttributes ; the attributes, 24 rows of 32

@module relocation
$EA0F w RelocTable 8 ; The offsets of the 67 relocatable words from the block's start, a zero word ends
> RelocateSelector $C0AB reads it once (c1: 68 words at frame 11841). The words listed are the block's own addresses: CALL, JP and LD operands; the stub, bank and paging-slot addresses are not in it.
$EA95 RelocTableEnd ; the zero word

@module leftovers ; Bytes no code uses
$EA97 b BlockTail 16 ; The block's last 97 bytes: attribute-like values ($68, $47, $43), never read
> Not SelectorPicture's attributes; no code reads them and no recording reads them (explorers reads $C708-$EA0E). Copied with the block (and 1000 bytes of set 0 after it) to RAM 1. Known only from the code.
